<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Introducing Panama to the World – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> International Communication</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> Introducing Panama to the World</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Comprehend complex narrative texts. Reading: Analyze persuasive pieces on current events.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can comprehend narratives that introduce Panama's global role, identifying main events (e.g., "In the story, Panama's strategic position in global trade is highlighted, showing how the Panama Canal plays a crucial role in connecting oceans and facilitating international commerce.") Can analyze persuasive elements in articles promoting Panama (e.g., "The article uses phrases like 'Panama is a key player in global trade' and 'a vital hub for international commerce' to emphasize Panama's global importance.") Can discuss themes of cultural pride in narratives about Panama (e.g., "The text promotes values of sustainability and conservation, celebrating Panama's biodiversity as a source of national pride and ecological importance.")</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a world map and points to Panama. Students are prompted to recall vocabulary from the previous lesson related to Panama's global role. The teacher asks, "What comes to mind when we talk about 'Panama' and 'international communication'?" Students quickly brainstorm keywords, which the teacher writes on the board (e.g., <i>trade, global, hub, canal, financial services, biodiversity</i>). This connects to the theme "Introducing Panama to the World" and activates prior knowledge.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models reading a short attention-grabbing headline or a key sentence related to Panama's global impact from a sample text, emphasizing target pronunciation. For example, the teacher reads: "WHAT MAKES PANama a KEY PLAYer in GLOBAL trade and ecoTOURism?" while explicitly stressing the Wh- question word 'WHAT' and highlighting the /t/ sound in 'trade' and the /g/ sound in 'global'. The teacher explains that today’s reading will focus on understanding texts that introduce Panama's global relevance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key terms or concepts used in the warm-up:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is <i>global trade</i> about local business or worldwide exchange of goods?" (Expected answer: Worldwide exchange.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When we say Panama is a <i>hub</i>, does it mean it's a small, isolated place or a central point of connection?" (Expected answer: A central point of connection.)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "International Communication"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher distributes an article excerpt titled "Panama: A Global Hub for Trade and Biodiversity" (B1.3 level). This text is a persuasive narrative promoting Panama's role on the international stage. It includes examples of passive voice ("Panama is recognized...", "The Canal was expanded..."), modals of necessity ("Panama must maintain its diverse ecosystems..."), and relative clauses ("Panama is a transit sector that connects continents..."). Key vocabulary (<i>trade, relevance, biodiversity, professionals, financial services, multinational, hub, canal, impact, sustainable, strategic, significant</i>) from the curriculum is prominently featured.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The students will develop skills in reading comprehension, specifically identifying main ideas, key details, and persuasive language within narrative and informational texts. This supports the ability to "comprehend complex narrative texts" and "analyze persuasive pieces."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the article quickly to identify the main topic of each paragraph. They match paragraphs to pre-written topic sentences (e.g., "Paragraph 1: Panama's Geographic Importance," "Paragraph 2: The Economic Power of the Canal").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> Students read specific statements about the text and mark them as True or False, correcting the false ones by citing evidence from the article (e.g., "Panama's strategic position in global trade is highlighted, showing how the Panama Canal plays a crucial role in connecting oceans and facilitating international commerce." -> True).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match key vocabulary words from the text (e.g., <i>commerce, ecotourist, multinational, relevance</i>) with their definitions or synonyms as used in context.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> This stage focuses on the accurate comprehension and analysis of the reading text, specifically targeting the grammatical structures (passive voice, modals of necessity, relative clauses) and the recommended vocabulary. Students will identify how these elements contribute to the text's message and persuasive intent.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Grammar Hunt:</b> Students re-read the article, highlighting examples of passive voice (e.g., "Panama is recognized for its trade influence," "new routes are being developed"), modals of necessity (e.g., "Panama must ensure sustainability"), and relative clauses (e.g., "Panama is a trade hub that connects continents"). The teacher then asks students to explain why these structures were used by the author (e.g., passive voice to emphasize the action/result, relative clauses to add detailed information).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Analyzing Persuasion:</b> In pairs, students identify phrases in the article that are used to *persuade* the reader about Panama's importance. They underline at least three persuasive phrases (e.g., "Panama is a key player in global trade," "a vital hub for international commerce," "its vibrant culture is attractive"). They discuss *why* these phrases are persuasive and *what* specific adjectives (<i>key, vital, vibrant</i>) contribute to that effect.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Summarizing Main Events & Themes:</b> Students work in pairs to discuss and list the main events or significant points highlighted in the narrative about Panama's global role. They also identify instances where the text promotes themes of cultural pride, sustainability, or conservation. For example, "The text promotes values of sustainability and conservation, celebrating Panama's biodiversity as a source of national pride and ecological importance." They compare their lists, justifying their choices with direct references to the text.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "International Communication"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative "Panama Briefing" presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Description:</b> In small groups (3-4 students), learners will act as "Panamanian International Representatives" who have just read the provided article. Their task is to prepare and deliver a short (2-3 minute) "Panama Briefing" to the class, summarizing the article's key points. The briefing must cover Panama's global role, mention main events or facts, analyze at least one persuasive element from the text, and briefly touch on a theme of cultural pride or importance mentioned in the reading. They should incorporate passive voice, relative clauses, and some of the target vocabulary learned (e.g., <i>strategic, biodiversity, financial services, hub, impact</i>) to effectively communicate the information from the text.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> This task directly simulates "International Communication" by having students present information about "Introducing Panama to the World" in a professional, structured manner based on their reading comprehension. Students will lead discussions, ensuring effective communication of viewpoints derived from the text (Sociolinguistic Competence).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in presenting the *comprehended* information, collaborative interaction within the group, and communicative task completion (i.e., successfully briefing the class about the article's content). The teacher encourages respectful language when discussing different interpretations of the text's message. (Pragmatic Competence).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Panama Briefing" presentations. Students will also complete a brief peer assessment for other groups based on their comprehension of the briefing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents their "Panama Briefing," demonstrating their ability to synthesize information from the complex narrative text, identify main events, and analyze persuasive elements. Students listen actively and use a simple checklist to evaluate how well other groups conveyed the main message and persuasive techniques from the article.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate comprehension of main events and Panama's global role from the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective identification and analysis of persuasive elements in the article.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of presentation and coherence of information derived from the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and equitable participation among group members.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., <i>strategic, relevance, financial services, biodiversity</i>) and grammatical structures (passive voice, relative clauses) to explain concepts from the reading.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts individually:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One important fact I comprehended about Panama's global role from today's text is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found it easy/challenging to analyze the persuasive language because..."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What reading strategies did I use today that helped me better comprehend the text about Panama's international relevance?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can identifying persuasive language help me evaluate information more critically in other subjects or in my daily life?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What aspects of reading comprehension (identifying main ideas, analyzing persuasive elements, understanding complex narratives) worked particularly well? Did students effectively use the target grammar (passive voice, relative clauses) and vocabulary in their discussions and presentations derived from the reading? Were the activities at the appropriate B1.3 CEFR level for 12th graders? What adjustments or scaffolds might be needed to deepen critical analysis of persuasive texts for the next reading task?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The content from this reading lesson, including the facts about Panama's global role and the persuasive language identified, will serve as the foundation for Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction). Students will use the information and arguments from the article to engage in discussions and debates about Panama's international standing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>